WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
There's an old saying:

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.700
God may have made the world,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.760
but the Dutch made the Netherlands.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.530
This is the Maeslant Barrier

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.500
and it's one part of the Delta Works,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.110
a set of megastructures
that hold back the ocean

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.540
when high seas threaten
to flood this country.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.310
This is one of the world's
largest movable structures,

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:20.807
a pair of 210-meter-long gates
designed to seal off

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:25.220
the main shipping route to Rotterdam port
and hold back storm surges.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:26.860
Depending on whose
measurements you believe,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.210
somewhere between a fifth and a third
of the Netherlands sits below sea level,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.010
a lot of that reclaimed
from the ocean over years

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.700
through managing marshlands
and moving earth and pumping water.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.800
There are areas of the Netherlands
described as "former islands",

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.550
and these gates are part
of the enormous system

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.880
that Dutch engineers built
to keep this country dry.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.140
And a couple of hours northeast of here
is where they learned to do it.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.740
This is just one part

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.880
of the 10 square kilometres
of Waterloopbos

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.690
and it was built when the
only way to simulate water

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.010
was to use water.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.500
It's now a national monument

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.450
and nature is taking
back these structures,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.647
these experiments.

00:01:08.647 --> 00:01:12.940
- Waterloop means how the
flow runs through a system,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.980
that is Waterloop in Dutch.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.030
Over here, we mostly did
what we call model studies,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.810
so basically simulating nature
at a certain, smaller scale.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.886
The whole area started more or less

00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:27.540
after the second World War,
so around 1950,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.950
when there was a big demand
for model studies related

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.250
to the Delta Works in the
southwest part of the Netherlands.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.410
It was for the Delta Works

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.403
but we also did many
studies all over the world,

00:01:37.403 --> 00:01:40.960
studies of the harbour of Libya,
harbours in Tunisia,

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.430
harbours in the Far East, Hong Kong.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.220
The main reason for building
the Waterloopbos here is

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.010
that we needed a lot of space
for the models

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:53.100
because, around 1950, all
the models were outdoor.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.920
And another thing is you need water.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.410
Where we are standing here is basically
the bottom of the former Zuiderzee.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.150
So we are here standing at the level,

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.440
let's say, minus two or minus three metres
below mean sea level.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.800
Here we have a natural water drop
of about five metres

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.350
so we just have to open the gate
at that part of the wood

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.430
and the water will flow
freely through this area.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:18.030
We are standing in what
we call the Delta flume,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.850
an enormous rectanglar box, so to say,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.280
and now you see more an artist's
impression of this flume

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:29.760
with certain sections cut out and placed
perpendicular to the original flume wall.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.770
In this flume, the studies are related
to the design of breakwaters.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.610
These breakwaters are enormously costly,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:40.700
in the order of, let's say,
100 million euros.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.560
If you take a section out
of your breakwater

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.300
and then scale it down
to about one to five,

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.770
and test it here.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.990
I've been in meetings where they fight
for every centimetre of the block.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.700
Well, if we propose, let's say,
that the blocks will be one metre,

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.100
they ask, could not be the block
95 centimetres

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.930
or 96 centimetre or 97 centimetres
because that is money.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.850
Each centimetre you could use
on a block is money

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.120
and therefore this was one
of the cash cows of Delta Hydraulics,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.180
doing these studies,
which are expensive,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.140
but still cheap in the
design of a big breakwater.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:16.620
At the moment, the Waterloopbos
is a kind of tourist attraction.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.140
The upcoming of the computer,
that was one of the main reasons

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.220
that this area was left around 2000.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.960
We do excursions for tourists

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.550
to see how we operated
the old, outdoor models

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.160
and this flume is handed over

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.900
to the natural monument
organisation about two years ago.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.270
So although we left the territory in 2000,

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.970
this flume was still
operational for about 15 years

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.970
because you cannot easily replace
this flume to another place.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.780
- The Water Act, part of Dutch law,
requires the government

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.290
to maintain flood defences.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.330
And it establishes a
maximum risk that's allowed.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.690
So there are parts of the Netherlands
that, in law, it must take

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.384
a one-in-10,000-year
weather event to flood.

00:04:01.384 --> 00:04:05.700
The trouble is that the predictions for
what "one in 10,000 years" involves

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.130
keep getting worse.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.740
Since they were completed in 1997,
the gates of the Maeslant Barrier

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.066
have only been closed in defence twice,

00:04:13.066 --> 00:04:15.810
but that's going to become
more common in future.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.000
Flood defences that were
researched here were designed

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.800
to protect the cities and towns
that people already lived in

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.110
but with those defences in place,
people kept building

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.990
and the consequences of
failure keep growing.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.490
The Dutch are going to have
to keep making the Netherlands

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.939
for a long time yet.

